--- !u!114 &2453025442325041136
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1860196466074616742}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 9f623ee2a26514a4d91aefdd3a055a6c, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  CanAutoPlay: 0
  Canvas: {fileID: 0}
  videoDataList:
  - time: 8
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Oh, hello there, young explorers. My name is Clayton, and as
      you can see, I'm having a bit of a rest today. I've kind of fallen apart.
  - time: 6
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: But don't worry, that's actually part of my story. Can you guess
      what type of rock I was once made of?
  - time: 4
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Let me give you a few clues. Clue one.
  - time: 13
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: I used to be a rock golem, just like Barry, Gracie, Leroy, Sally,
      and Marvin. But over thousands and thousands of years, I broke down into teeny
      tiny particles, much smaller than sand. Clue two.
  - time: 8
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Now I'm made of particles so small you can barely see them.
      When I get wet, I feel sticky and smooth, like Play-Doh or modeling clay. Clue
      three.
  - time: 14
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: I hold on to water really, really well, sometimes too well.
      When it rains, I can get soggy and heavy, which is why I'm having trouble standing
      up right now. Clue four.
  - time: 15
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: I'm not really a rock anymore. I've become something that plants
      can grow in. I've transformed into soil. Have you guessed? I'm made of clay
      soil. That's right, I'm Clayton the Clay Soil Golem. My story, From Rock to
      Soil.
  - time: 12
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Here's what happened to me. I used to be a proper rock golem.
      But over millions of years, something called weathering happened to me.
  - time: 32
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Let me explain. Weathering is when rocks slowly break down into
      smaller and pieces because of rain washing over them, wind blowing against
      them, ice freezing and cracking them, plant roots growing into cracks, hot
      sun and cold nights making them expand and shrink, rivers and streams tumbling
      them around. Over a very long time, big rocks become small rocks, small rocks
      become pebbles, pebbles become grains, and grains become tiny particles. Eventually
      rock becomes soil.
  - time: 11
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: So I'm what happens when rocks have been weathered for so long
      that they've turned into super fine particles mixed with bits of dead plants
      and tiny creatures. I'm still made of rock, just really broken down rock.
  - time: 29
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: So what is soil? Soil is a mixture of tiny rock particles from
      weathered rocks like me, dead plant and animal bits called organic matter or
      humus, water, air, tiny living creatures like worms and bugs. Soil is super
      important because it's where plants grow and plants give us food, oxygen and
      homes for animals. The six different types of soil.
  - time: 12
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Now, I'm made of clay, but there are actually six main types
      of soil. Each one is different because they're made of different sized particles
      and have different amounts of nutrients.
  - time: 65
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: One, clay soil. That's me. Particle size. The tiniest particles.
      You can barely see them. Texture. When dry, I'm hard and lumpy. When wet, I'm
      sticky, smooth and heavy, like modeling clay or Play-Doh. Water. I hold on
      to water really well, maybe too well. I get waterlogged easily and drain very
      slowly. That's why I've collapsed into a puddle today. Color. Usually reddish
      brown, brown or gray. Good points. I'm full of nutrients that plants love.
      I hold water well, so plants don't dry out quickly. I'm good for making pottery
      and bricks. Tricky points. I get waterlogged and heavy when wet. I dry into
      hard clumps in hot weather. Plant roots can have trouble pushing through me.
      I warm up slowly and spring best for growing. Plants that like wet conditions,
      but I need organic matter added to help me drain better.
  - time: 58
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Two, sandy soil. My cousin. Particle size. Big particles you
      can see easily, like my friend Sally Sandstone, broken down. Texture. Gritty
      and rough, just like beach sand. If you rub it between your fingers, it feels
      scratchy and won't stick together. Water. Sandy soil is the opposite of me.
      Water drains through it super fast, almost too fast. It's like a sieve or colander.
      Color. Usually light brown, yellow or pale. Good points. Easy to dig and work
      with, warms up quickly in spring, doesn't get waterlogged. Plant roots can
      push through easily. Tricky points. Water drains away too quickly. Nutrients
      wash away with the water. Plants can dry out easily. Needs lots of watering
      and feeding, best for growing carrots, potatoes and plants that like dry conditions.
  - time: 53
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Three, silty soil. The middle child. Particle size. Medium sized.
      Bigger than clay but smaller than sand. Texture. Silky and smooth like flour
      or talcum powder. It feels soft and soapy when wet. Water. Holds water better
      than sand but drains better than clay. It's right in the middle. Color. Usually
      dark brown or gray brown. Good points. Full of nutrients. Holds moisture well
      but drains better than clay. Soft and easy to work with. Feels lovely and silky.
      Tricky points. Can wash away easily in heavy rain, called erosion. Can get
      compacted, squashed down, easily. Might not have great structure. Best for
      growing. Lots of vegetables and plants love silty soil.
  - time: 58
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Four, peaty soil. The spongy one. What it's made of. Lots and
      lots of dead plant material. Peat forms in wetlands and bogs where plants don't
      fully decompose. Rot away. Texture. Spongy, dark and full of organic matter.
      Like a damp sponge. Water. Holds tons of water, even more than me. It's like
      a sponge that soaks everything up. Color. Very dark brown or almost black.
      Good points. Super rich in nutrients and organic matter. Excellent for growing
      plants. Retains lots of moisture. Acidic, which some plants love. Tricky points.
      Can be too acidic for some plants. Rare and needs to be protected. We shouldn't
      harvest too much peat from nature. Can be quite wet and heavy. Best for growing.
      Acid loving plants like blueberries, rhododendrons, and heathers.
  - time: 51
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Five, chalky soil. The alkaline one. What it's made of. Contains
      lots of calcium carbonate. Remember Leroy the limestone golem? He's in here.
      Also contains lots of stones and lumps. Texture. Stony, lumpy, and often pale
      colored. You'll find lots of white stones mixed in. Water. Drains quickly like
      sandy soil. Color. Pale, often light brown, gray, or even whitish. Good points.
      Good drainage. Some plants love alkaline, the opposite of acidic soil. Warms
      up quickly. Tricky points. Can be too alkaline for many plants. Nutrients can
      be locked up and hard for plants to use. Often shallow and stony. Iron can
      be lacking, making plants turn yellow. Best for growing. Lilac, lavender, and
      plants that like alkaline conditions.
  - time: 64
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Six, loamy soil. The perfect one. What it's made of. A beautiful
      mixture of clay, sand, and silt. The best of all three. Plus lots of organic
      matter. Particle size. A mix of all sizes. Texture. Crumbly, soft, and moist
      like chocolate cake. It holds together but also breaks apart easily. Water.
      Perfect. It holds moisture but also drains well. Not too wet. Not too dry.
      Color. Rich, dark brown. Good points. Gardeners call this the perfect soil.
      Full of nutrients. Great drainage and water retention. Easy to work with. Perfect
      structure for plant roots. Warm in spring. Cool in summer. Tricky points. Hard
      to find naturally. Most gardeners have to create loamy soil by mixing different
      types together and adding compost. Best for growing. Almost everything. Most
      vegetables, flowers, and plants love loamy soil.
  - time: 56
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: How to remember the six soil types. Here's a fun way to remember
      us all. Clayton the clay. Tiny, sticky, holds water. Sandy soil. Gritty, drains
      fast. Silty soil. Smooth and silky. Peaty peat. Dark, spongy, organic. Chalky
      chalk. Pale, stony, alkaline. Loamy loam. Perfect mix of everything. Testing
      soil. The squeeze test. Want to know what type of soil you have? Try the squeeze
      test. Take some moist soil in your hand. Squeeze it tight into a ball. Open
      your hand and see what happens. Forms a hard ball that doesn't break. Probably
      clay, like me. Falls apart immediately. Probably sandy. Forms a ball but breaks
      easily. Probably silty or loamy. Dark and spongy. Probably peaty. Pale with
      white lumps. Probably chalky.
  - time: 51
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Why soil is so important. Even though I've fallen apart, I'm
      actually more useful now than when I was a rock. Plants grow in soil. And plants
      give us food, oxygen, and beauty. Tiny creatures live in soil. Worms, bugs,
      bacteria, and fungi all help break down dead things and make nutrients. Soil
      filters water. As rain soaks through, soil cleans it. Soil stores carbon. This
      helps fight climate change. We build on soil. Houses, roads, and cities sit
      on top of soil.
  - time: 51
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: So remember, young geologists, I'm Clayton the Clay Soil Golem.
      I used to be solid rock like Barry, Gracie, Leroy, Sally, and Marvin. But over
      millions of years, weathering broke me down into teeny tiny clay particles.
      Now I'm soil. And even though I've collapsed, I'm super important for growing
      plants and supporting life. The six soil types are clay, me, sand, silt, peat,
      chalk, and loam. Each one is special and different. Made from different sized
      rock particles and different amounts of organic matter. Every rock eventually
      becomes soil. And soil helps new life grow. It's the amazing circle of geology
      and life on Earth. Pretty cool, isn't it? Now you understand the whole story.
      From igneous rocks born in fire, to sedimentary rocks made in layers, to metamorphic
      rocks that changed, to minerals that make up rocks, and finally to soil where
      rocks end up after millions of years of weathering. You're not just rock experts
      anymore. You're soil scientists, too.
  displayImage: {fileID: 0}
  displayText: {fileID: 0}
  SubtitleCanvas: {fileID: 0}
  animator: {fileID: 7676624757924936528}
  audioSource: {fileID: 1456984080289920849}
  AmbiantAudioSource: {fileID: 0}
