--- !u!114 &3077865253581349470
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 2180245627295782072}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 9f623ee2a26514a4d91aefdd3a055a6c, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  CanAutoPlay: 0
  Canvas: {fileID: 0}
  videoDataList:
  - time: 13
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: G'day, young rock detectives. My name is Leroy and I'm a very
      different kind of golem from Barry and Gracie. Can you guess what type of rock
      I'm made of? Let me give you a few clues.
  - time: 11
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Clue 1. I'm usually pale and light-colored. White, cream, gray,
      or sometimes a soft tan color. I might remind you of chalk or seashells.
  - time: 15
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Clue 2. I wasn't made from hot melted rock like Barry and Gracie
      were. Instead, I was made at the bottom of the ocean. Tiny bits and pieces
      piled up layer by layer over millions of years to create me.
  - time: 18
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Clue 3. Look really closely at me. Can you see what looks like
      tiny shell pieces or fossils? That's because I'm actually made from ancient
      sea creatures. When they died, their shells settled to the bottom of the sea
      and eventually became... me.
  - time: 15
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Clue 4. I'm softer than Barry and Gracie. You could even scratch
      me with a coin. And here's something really cool. If you put vinegar on me,
      I fizz and bubble like soft drink.
  - time: 5
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Have you guessed it? I'm made of limestone. That's right! Limestone!
  - time: 16
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Now here's where I'm really different from Barry and Gracie.
      They're both igneous rocks made from hot melted rock. But I'm a sedimentary
      rock. That means I was made from layers and layers of sediment, tiny pieces
      that got squashed together over a very long time.
  - time: 27
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Here's what makes me special. Where I come from. Millions of
      years ago, I formed at the bottom of warm shallow seas and oceans. When tiny
      sea creatures like coral, shellfish, and plankton died, their shells and skeletons
      sank to the bottom. Over millions of years, these piled up in layers, got squashed
      by all the weight above them, and slowly turned into solid rock. Me.
  - time: 11
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: My color. I'm usually light colored. White, cream, gray, or
      tan. Sometimes I can have pretty patterns or stripes from all those different
      layers.
  - time: 15
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: What I'm made of? I'm made of something called calcium carbonate.
      The same stuff that's in seashells, snail shells, and even the pearls in oysters.
      That's why I sometimes look like I have tiny shell pieces in me.
  - time: 13
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: My texture. I can feel smooth and fine, or sometimes rough and
      chalky. Some types of limestone are so soft, they crumble a bit in your hands.
      I'm nowhere near as hard as granite.
  - time: 15
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: My fossil treasures. Because I formed in ancient seas, I often
      have fossils inside me. You might find ancient seashells, coral, or even sea
      creature skeletons preserved in limestone. It's like being a time capsule.
  - time: 17.5
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: My fizzy secret. Here's my coolest trick. If you drop vinegar
      or lemon juice on limestone, we fizz and bubble. That's because the acid reacts
      with the calcium carbonate inside us. Scientists use this test to identify
      limestone rocks.
  - time: 17
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Where you can find me. I'm found all over the world. There are
      huge limestone cliffs by the sea, limestone caves with stalactites and stalagmites,
      and even famous buildings made for me. The great pyramids in Egypt are partly
      made of limestone.
  - time: 11
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: What humans use me for. Building blocks for houses and buildings,
      making cement and concrete, crushed up for roads. Ground up to make chalk for
      your classroom.
  - time: 6.5
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Even crushed limestone is used to help farmers' soil grow better
      crops.
  - time: 16.5
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Cool fact. Some limestone caves have amazing formations like
      stalactites, hanging from the ceiling like icicles, and stalagmites, growing
      up from the floor. These form when water with dissolved limestone drips for
      thousands of years.
  - time: 14
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: So remember, young geologists, I'm Leroy the Limestone Golem,
      born from ancient sea creatures made of layers, and I fizz with vinegar. I'm
      a sedimentary rock, which means I tell the story of life from millions of years
      ago.
  - time: 18
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: See how different I am from Barry and Gracie? Igneous rocks
      come from fire and heat, but sedimentary rocks like me come from layers of
      sediment getting squashed together over time. We each have our own special
      story.
  displayImage: {fileID: 0}
  displayText: {fileID: 0}
  SubtitleCanvas: {fileID: 0}
  animator: {fileID: 8700933485721039797}
  audioSource: {fileID: 2228107188071940696}
  AmbiantAudioSource: {fileID: 0}
  TalkingAnim:
  - {fileID: -203655887218126122, guid: 0e9551bc6cd60f04d97113009568c675, type: 3}
  - {fileID: -203655887218126122, guid: 5fee6e4c39a7c8548a4aad93d5ef8a21, type: 3}
  - {fileID: -203655887218126122, guid: 0d557617e530e3b4293fc607895e85e0, type: 3}
  IdleState: Idle
  WaveState: Wave
  TalkingState: Talking
  WaveDuration: 2
