--- !u!114 &8746069312130423821
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 5326319752441490759}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 9f623ee2a26514a4d91aefdd3a055a6c, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  CanAutoPlay: 0
  Canvas: {fileID: 0}
  videoDataList:
  - time: 4
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Oh, hello there, young explorers. My name is Clayton, and as
      you can see,
  - time: 5
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: I'm having a bit of a rest today. I've kind of fallen apart.
  - time: 7
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: But don't worry, that's actually part of my story. Can you guess
      what type of rock I was once made of? Let me give you a few clues.
  - time: 13
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Clue one. I used to be a rock golem, just like Barry, Gracie,
      Leroy, Sally, and Marvin. But over thousands and thousands of years, I broke
      down into teeny tiny particles, much smaller than sand.
  - time: 13
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Clue two. Now I'm made of particles so small you can barely
      see them. When I get wet, I feel sticky and smooth, like Play-Doh or modeling
      clay.
  - time: 12
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Clue three. I hold on to water really, really well, sometimes
      too well. When it rains, I can get soggy and heavy, which is why I'm having
      trouble standing up right now.
  - time: 5
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Clue four. I'm not really a rock anymore. I've become something
      that plants can grow in. I've transformed into soil.
  - time: 8
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Have you guessed? I'm made of clay soil. That's right, I'm Clayton
      the Clay Soil Golem.
  - time: 11
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: My story, from rock to soil. Here's what happened to me. I used
      to be a proper rock golem, but over millions of years, something called weathering
      happened to me.
  - time: 16
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Weathering is when rocks slowly break down into smaller and
      smaller pieces because of rain washing over them, wind blowing against them,
      ice freezing and cracking them, plant roots growing into cracks, hot sun and
      cold nights making them expand and shrink, and rivers and streams tumbling
      them around.
  - time: 12
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Over a very long time, big rocks become small rocks, small rocks
      become pebbles, pebbles become grains, and grains become tiny particles.
  - time: 11
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Eventually rock becomes soil. I'm what happens when rocks have
      been weathered for so long that they've turned into super fine particles mixed
      with bits of dead plants and tiny creatures. I'm still made of rock, just really
      broken down rock.
  - time: 23
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: So what is soil? Soil is a mixture of tiny rock particles from
      weathered rocks like me, dead plant and animal bits called organic matter or
      humus, water, air, and tiny living creatures like worms and bugs.
  - time: 13
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Soil is super important because it's where plants grow, and
      plants give us food, oxygen, and homes for animals.
  - time: 15
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: The six different types of soil. Now I'm made of clay, but there
      are actually six main types of soil. Each one is different because they're
      made of different sized particles and have different amounts of nutrients.
  - time: 49
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Clay soil. That's me. The tiniest particles you can barely see.
      When dry, I'm hard and lumpy. When wet, I'm sticky, smooth, and heavy like
      modeling clay or Play-Doh. I hold on to water really well, maybe too well.
      I get waterlogged easily and drain very slowly. I'm usually reddish brown,
      brown, or gray. I'm full of nutrients plants love, good for pottery and bricks,
      but tricky because I get heavy, dry into hard clumps, roots struggle, and I
      warm up slowly. Best for plants that like wet conditions, with organic matter
      added.
  - time: 60
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Sandy soil, my cousin. Big particles you can see easily, gritty
      and rough like beach sand. Water drains through super fast like a sieve. Usually
      light brown or pale. Easy to dig, warms quickly, roots grow easily, but nutrients
      wash away fast and plants dry out. Best for carrots, potatoes, and dry-loving
      plants.
  - time: 52
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Silty soil, the middle child. Medium sized particles, silky
      and smooth like flour. Holds water better than sand but drains better than
      clay. Dark brown or gray brown, full of nutrients, easy to work with, but can
      erode and compact easily. Great for vegetables and many plants.
  - time: 58
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Peaty soil, the spongy one. Made of lots of dead plant material
      from wetlands and bogs. Dark, spongy, and full of organic matter. Holds tons
      of water and is very nutrient rich. Acidic, which some plants love, but too
      acidic for others and needs protecting. Best for blueberries, rhododendrons,
      and heathers.
  - time: 57
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Chalky soil, the alkaline one. Contains calcium carbonate and
      lots of stones. Pale, stony, drains quickly, warms fast, but nutrients can
      be locked up and iron can be lacking. Best for lilac, lavender, and alkaline-loving
      plants.
  - time: 56
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Loamy soil, the perfect one. A mixture of clay, sand, and silt
      with organic matter. Crumbly, moist, chocolate-cake texture. Holds water but
      drains well. Dark brown, full of nutrients, easy to work with. Hard to find
      naturally, but perfect for almost everything.
  - time: 62
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: How to remember the six soil types and the squeeze test. Squeeze
      moist soil into a ball and see what happens to identify clay, sandy, silty,
      peaty, chalky, or loamy soil.
  - time: 49
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Why soil is so important. Plants grow in soil, tiny creatures
      live in it, soil filters water, stores carbon, and supports buildings and cities.
  - time: 46
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: "Final recap. I'm Clayton the Clay Soil Golem. Every rock eventually
      becomes soil, soil supports life, and now you're not just rock experts anymore
      \u2014 you're soil scientists too."
