--- !u!114 &700543548919710270
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 7284665856812522544}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 9f623ee2a26514a4d91aefdd3a055a6c, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  CanAutoPlay: 0
  Canvas: {fileID: 0}
  videoDataList:
  - time: 11
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Hello there, young rock explorers. My name is Steve, and I'm
      a very layered kind of golem. I have a lot in common with my friends Leroy
      and Sally. Can you guess what type of rock I am made of?
  - time: 15
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Let me give you a few clues. Clue 1. I'm usually gray or dark
      gray in color, but I can also be black, brown, or even reddish. I look smooth
      and dull, not shiny or sparkly at all.
  - time: 14
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Clue 2. Look closely at me. Can you see all those thin flat
      layers? I'm made up of hundreds or thousands of super thin sheets stacked on
      top of each other, like the pages in a book.
  - time: 24
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Clue 3. Like Leroy and Sally, I'm also a sedimentary rock, but
      I'm made from even tinier particles than Sally's sand, particles of mud and
      clay that settled at the bottom of quiet, calm water. Clue 4. Here's my special
      trick. I can split really easily along my layers. If you tap me just right,
      I'll break into thin flat pieces like sheets of paper.
  - time: 7.5
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: It's like I'm a rock that wants to be a book. Have you guessed
      it yet? I'm made of shale. That's right.
  - time: 14
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Shale. Just like my friends Leroy the Limestone and Sally the
      Sandstone, I'm a sedimentary rock. But while Leroy is made from sea creatures
      and Sally is made from sand, I'm made from something even finer. Mud and clay.
  - time: 33
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: That makes me special. Where I come from. Imagine the very bottom
      of a calm, quiet lake or deep in the ocean where the water is still and peaceful.
      Tiny, tiny particles of mud and clay, much, much smaller than sand, slowly
      drift down through the water and settle on the bottom. Layer after layer after
      layer for thousands and thousands of years. Over time, all those layers of
      mud get buried under more and more sediment. The weight squashes them down
      and water is slowly squeezed out. Eventually, all that soft mud becomes hard
      rock. Me.
  - time: 14
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Each thin layer represents a different time. Maybe a different
      season, year, or even thousands of years.
  - time: 21
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: My fine texture. I'm made of the smallest particles, even tinier
      than clay. My particles are so small you can't see or feel them individually
      like you can with Sally's sand. When you touch me, I feel smooth and dull,
      not gritty like sandstone and not as rough as limestone. I'm fine-grained,
      which means my particles are teeny tiny.
  - time: 35
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: My amazing layers. This is what makes me really special. I'm
      made up of thin, flat layers stacked on top of each other. These layers are
      called bedding planes. Think of me like a deck of cards or the pages in a book.
      Lots of thin sheets all pressed together. Because of these layers, I have a
      special property called facility. Say it, facility. This fancy word just means
      I split easily along my layers. If you hit me with a hammer or apply pressure
      in the right way, I'll break into thin, flat pieces, not into random chunks
      like other rocks.
  - time: 25
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: My colors. I'm usually gray or dark gray, most common. Black,
      if I have lots of organic matter, dead plants, in me. Brown or reddish, if
      I have iron, rust, in me. Green or greenish gray, if I have certain minerals
      in me. My color tells you what was in the mud when I
  - time: 24
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: My strength, or lack of it. I'm quite soft for a rock. I'm softer
      than Sally Sandstone, and much softer than the igneous rocks like Barry and
      Gracie. In fact, you can scratch me with your fingernail or a coin. I break
      apart easily, especially along my layers. I crumble when exposed to weather
      for a long time. Water can make me swell up and become even weaker, where you
      can find me.
  - time: 22
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: I'm actually one of the most common sedimentary rocks on earth.
      You can find me in hillsides with visible layers, along riverbanks and cliffs,
      in quarries and road cuts, under the ground in many places, near ancient lake
      beds and ocean floors. I'm especially common in areas that used to be covered
      by ancient seas or large lakes millions of years ago.
  - time: 28
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: My fossil treasures. Because I formed in quiet water where mud
      settled, I often have amazing fossils. I can preserve ancient fish, trilobites,
      ancient sea creatures, plant leaves and impressions, shells, even dinosaur
      footprints sometimes. The fine mud that made me was perfect for capturing and
      preserving delicate details, so fossils in shale can be really well preserved
      and beautiful.
  - time: 51
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: What humans use me for. Even though I'm soft and break easily,
      humans still find me useful. Source of oil and gas. This is my biggest use.
      Deep underground, some types of shale contain oil and natural gas trapped inside
      me. Humans drill down to get this energy out. It's called shale gas or shale
      oil. Making bricks and ceramics. I can be heated up and turned into bricks
      for building or made into pottery and ceramics. Making slate. If I get squeezed
      and heated underground, metamorphosed like Marvin, I can turn into slate, those
      smooth, flat rocks used for roofing tiles and chalkboards. Road construction,
      crushed up shale can be used as fill material for roads and foundations. Cement.
      Production. I'm sometimes mixed with limestone to make cement.
  - time: 36
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: How I'm different from other sedimentary rocks. Let me explain
      because this is important. Sally, sandstone, is made from sand, big particles
      you can see and feel. She feels gritty and rough. Leroy, limestone, is made
      from sea creature shells and skeletons. He fizzes with vinegar. Me, Steve,
      shale, is made from mud and clay, the tiniest particles. I feel smooth and
      split into layers easily. We're all sedimentary rocks, but we're made from
      different sizes of particles.
  - time: 29
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: The particle size scale. Here's how it works from biggest to
      smallest. Boulders and pebbles, when stuck together, become conglomerate rock.
      Sand grains, when stuck together, become sandstone. Sally. Silt, tiny grains,
      when stuck together, become siltstone. Clay and mud, tiniest particles, when
      stuck together, become shale. Me. I'm at the finest end of the scale, made
      from the smallest particles.
  - time: 32
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: Cool fact about my layers. Scientists can study my layers to
      learn about Earth's history. Each layer tells a story. Thin, light-colored
      layers might mean a dry season with less mud. Thick, dark layers might mean
      lots of plants died and washed into the water. Layers with different colors
      show different conditions when I formed. The thickness and number of layers
      can tell scientists how long it took for me to form. I'm like a history book
      written in stone.
  - time: 26
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: My weakness. Weathering. Here's something important about me.
      I don't last very long when exposed to weather. Rain soaks into me and makes
      me swell up. Freezing water inside me cracks me apart. My layers peel off like
      the pages of a wet book. I crumble and turn back into mud over time. That's
      why you don't often see shale cliffs or mountains. I weather away too quickly.
  - time: 36
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: But deep underground, protected from weather, I can last for
      millions of years. My transformation. Becoming slate. Remember Marvin the Marble
      Golem, who used to be Leroy the Limestone? Well, the same thing can happen
      to me. If I get buried really deep underground where it's hot, and get squeezed
      by lots of pressure, I can transform into slate. When I become slate, my layers
      get even tighter and harder. I become much stronger and more durable. I can
      be split into perfect flat sheets. I'm used for roofing tiles, chalkboards,
      and decorative stone.
  - time: 51
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: So shale, me, plus heat, plus pressure, equals slate. Metamorphic
      rock. Why I'm important. Even though I'm soft and crumble easily, I'm actually
      super important. Energy source. Much of the world's oil and natural gas comes
      from shale. Deep underground, ancient plants and sea creatures got trapped
      in my mud layers, and over millions of years, they turned into oil and gas.
      History. Keeper. My layers and fossils tell scientists about ancient climates,
      environments, and life on Earth millions of years ago. Building materials.
      I can be turned into bricks, ceramics, and cement. Soil formation. When I weather
      and crumble, I help create nutrient-rich soil that plants love.
  - time: 30
    image: {fileID: 0}
    textInformation: So remember, young geologists, I'm Steve the Shale Golem, made
      from ancient mud and clay that settled in quiet water, layered like a book,
      and I split easily. I'm a sedimentary rock that tells stories about Earth's
      past and helps provide energy for the present. Now you've met sedimentary rocks
      made from sea creatures, Leroy, sand, sally, and mud, me. Each of us formed
      from different types of sediment, and we all have our own special properties
      and stories to tell. Want to meet more of my rocky friends on your geological
      adventure?
  displayImage: {fileID: 0}
  displayText: {fileID: 0}
  SubtitleCanvas: {fileID: 0}
  animator: {fileID: 9049919732040116415}
  audioSource: {fileID: 4705307846686141316}
  AmbiantAudioSource: {fileID: 0}
  TalkingAnim:
  - {fileID: -203655887218126122, guid: 0e9551bc6cd60f04d97113009568c675, type: 3}
  - {fileID: -203655887218126122, guid: 5fee6e4c39a7c8548a4aad93d5ef8a21, type: 3}
  - {fileID: -203655887218126122, guid: 0d557617e530e3b4293fc607895e85e0, type: 3}
  IdleState: Idle
  WaveState: Wave
  TalkingState: Talking
  WaveDuration: 2
